@doc """
Standard `Applicative` trait declaration.

`Applicative` extends `Functor` with two operations:

- `pure` lifts an ordinary value into the context.
- `ap` applies a function already in the context to a value in the same
  context.

The `|*|` operator is the surface form of `Applicative::ap`:
`mapper |*| value`. The mapper must be explicitly curried when it has more
than one argument, so each `|*|` step consumes one contextual argument.

For example, `Ok(curry(&Add::add)) |*| Ok(1) |*| Ok(2)` evaluates to
`Ok(3)`. `Applicative` preserves the container's failure or short-circuit
behavior while combining successful values.

## REPL

xldr(1)> Ok(curry(&Add::add)) |*| Ok(1) |*| Ok(2)
Ok(3)
"""
@autoimport
deftrait Applicative
where
  Self: Functor
{
  # Lift a plain value into the context. This is the applicative identity
  # used when a computation needs a contextual value without effects.
  def pure::<Self>(value: $A) -> Self<$A>

  # Apply a contextual callable to a contextual value. With a curried mapper,
  # repeated calls consume arguments from left to right.
  def ap(mapper: Self<($A -> $B)>, value: Self<$A>) -> Self<$B>
}
